WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.120
Adobe finally got caught.
They are now paying $150&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:12.240
million, and I think you know why.
But, something even weirder is going on.&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:19.600
Adobe has been making more money than ever.&nbsp;
Their revenue has nearly quintupled since 2015.&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:25.360
But their stock has fallen&nbsp;
70% to an eight year low.&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:33.360
Their market cap just dropped below $100 billion.
And their CEO of 18 years just resigned.&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.560
What happened?&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:50.240
And, why is everyone betting against Adobe,&nbsp;
when they're making more money than ever?

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:55.600
Over the years, Adobe built the most&nbsp;
dominant position in creative software.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:02.640
Photoshop. Illustrator. Premiere. Acrobat.&nbsp;
There is no company that rivals their position.&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.400
Graphic design, video editing, photography,&nbsp;
audio mixing, anything creative.&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:15.360
But maintaining a monopoly takes more than great&nbsp;
software, which brings us to CEO Shantanu Narayen.&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:22.480
In 2013, Adobe was about to change everything.
Adobe had a very specific strategy for that.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:27.360
Narayen pushed Adobe to move from&nbsp;
boxed sales, to the subscription model.&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.760
"Today, people buy experiences, not products,"
Their revenue began to soar, as they added&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.600
hundreds of millions to every quarter.
And on the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:43.840
this might seem like a win win.
Adobe products used to be pricey, around $700, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:49.680
the entire creative suite might be a few thousand.
...so what was the problem? You save a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:55.840
of money subscribing each month, right?
Well, for one, when you bought you owned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:03.440
that copy of Photoshop. That $700 wasn't for&nbsp;
a year of use, it was totally yours. You could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.840
upgrade to the newest version for $200 as well.
You would pick up a physical disc in-store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:13.280
install it, and you were good to go.
But, that subscription of Adobe's…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.760
wasn't actually saving you much money.
Adobe didn't offer two options like most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.680
subscription software. They offered three.
The monthly plan — most expensive. The annual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.800
plan, paid upfront. And a third, in the&nbsp;
middle, right where your eyes go first.&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:36.720
A monthly plan, but cheaper? Except, it's&nbsp;
not monthly. It's annually, billed monthly.&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.400
You were locked into a year-long&nbsp;
plan, charged each month.&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.800
And it gets worse.
If you wanted to cancel, Adobe charged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:51.360
you 50% of every remaining month on your contract.
Why was someone who doesn’t even want to pay for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.000
one month supposed to pay&nbsp;
half of every month left?&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Some users were trapped the moment&nbsp;
their 14-day free trial ended.&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.280
You might be paying $20 a month,&nbsp;
but cancelling could be $70.&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.640
But it gets even worse.
Many subscriptions allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:15.440
you to turn off "auto-renew", so your plan will&nbsp;
automatically cancel at the end. Adobe doesn't.&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:21.360
You cannot disable your plan auto-renewing.&nbsp;
The only way is to cancel the entire plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.440
which causes the massive cancellation fee.
It doesn't help that customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:30.560
service was notoriously unreliable.
If you go to the Adobe community website,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:36.000
Reddit, or any other creative forum, you'll&nbsp;
see endless posts about cancellation fees.&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.680
"I've tried to cancel every month and they&nbsp;
still charge me. I tried to contact them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.400
directly and the phones don't work, tried to&nbsp;
cancel through PayPal and it doesn't work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.520
Emailed them directly, they don't respond&nbsp;
and proceed to charge my account again."

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:57.600
"I'm trying to cancel my Adobe Acrobat Pro,&nbsp;
but I can't afford the termination fee."&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:04.000
"Adobe doubled my plan then hit me with a $200&nbsp;
cancellation fee. Freaking out right now."&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.280
The only reliable way to avoid it, from what&nbsp;
I've seen, was to just cancel your card.&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:15.680
In some cases, users even had Adobe charge them —&nbsp;
multiple times — after they'd already cancelled.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.920
Customers hated Adobe, yet it felt like there&nbsp;
were no alternatives for most of their products.&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:27.040
Sure you could find an alternative, but if you&nbsp;
worked in design, marketing or a creative field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:33.360
your employer likely wanted you to use Adobe.
It's what everyone else was using, and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.640
were already subscribed to Creative Cloud, so…
Which brings us back to CEO Shantanu Narayen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.600
and, a weird twist in this story?&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:46.720
Narayen has, consistently, been ranked&nbsp;
as one of the best CEOs to work for.&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:56.000
He was #1 in 2021, #3 in 2025, is in the&nbsp;
top 5% on Comparably with an A+, or 93/100.&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.000
So while they weren't great to customers, he took&nbsp;
care of employees and shareholders extremely well.&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.680
Nonetheless, Adobe was the undisputed King.&nbsp;
And it's partly why they're making more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:15.280
money than ever today… but not the only reason.
But, soon they'd have to answer for their crimes.&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:18.880
The endless complaints were adding up.
They'd caught the attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.800
of someone much bigger.
But in the meantime, Adobe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:36.160
had other matters to attend to. They were about to&nbsp;
drop billions in a fight to keep their monopoly.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:41.200
Many fees and costs of business are&nbsp;
hugely confusing. Yet what can be even&nbsp;&nbsp;

