00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.680
On September 15, 2022, Adobe announced&nbsp;
it was acquiring Figma. $20 billion.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.440
Why Figma?&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:43.840
Figma had something rare. Real-time collaboration.&nbsp;
Lightweight. Browser-based. No install required.&nbsp;

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.640
Plus, a very generous free tier, compared&nbsp;
to Adobe's oppressive hidden fees.&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:54.960
It was the most credible threat to Adobe's design&nbsp;
dominance in years, and it was growing fast.&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.920
Users had watched Adobe acquire&nbsp;
their favorite tools over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:06.240
years — and slowly watch them change or disappear.
In 2005, Adobe acquired the design tool FreeHand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:13.040
Then, slowly killed development, and shut it down.
Why? It was a competitor to Illustrator.&nbsp;

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:18.800
Then, when users got mad, they said "While we&nbsp;
recognize FreeHand has a loyal customer base,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.560
we encourage users to migrate&nbsp;
to the new Adobe Illustrator".&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:29.440
So, fans of Figma knew exactly what was coming.
But this time, it didn't go as planned.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:35.120
Out of nowhere, Adobe abandoned the acquisition.
They had run into some trouble.&nbsp;

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:42.080
Previous Adobe acquisitions had mostly passed&nbsp;
unnoticed. But this was big. $20 billion.&nbsp;

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:47.360
So, it stretched across the US and Europe.
The UK's Competition and Markets Authority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.960
opened an in-depth investigation, publishing&nbsp;
a provisional finding of competition concerns.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:58.800
They said it would "harm innovation for software&nbsp;
used by the vast majority of UK digital designers"&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.200
The European Commission followed in&nbsp;
November 2023, with similar concerns.&nbsp;

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:07.520
So, Adobe and Figma walked&nbsp;
away on December 18, 2023.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:13.280
Though, this meant Adobe had to pay a "reverse&nbsp;
breakup fee". Aka, a fee in mergers to protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:20.560
the smaller company. In this case, Figma.
But for Adobe, this was $1 billion dollars.&nbsp;

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:27.840
That's right. Adobe had to pay a $1 billion&nbsp;
dollar cancellation fee. You love to see it.&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:32.800
More than the financial hit, it was a huge&nbsp;
threat to their monopoly. They had literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:38.800
handed $1 billion to their biggest competitor.
But that wasn't even the biggest problem.&nbsp;

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:43.120
While the Figma deal was collapsing,&nbsp;
the FTC had been building a case.&nbsp;

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:47.600
Then, they filed a lawsuit.
"[Adobe] fails to adequately disclose to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:52.560
consumers that by signing up for the 'Annual, Paid&nbsp;
Monthly' subscription plan, they are agreeing to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:58.640
year-long commitment and a hefty early termination&nbsp;
fee [ETF] that can amount to hundreds of dollars."&nbsp;

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:03.680
They also said "Adobe's ETF disclosures&nbsp;
are buried on the company's website in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:08.720
small print or require consumers to hover&nbsp;
over small icons to find the disclosures."&nbsp;

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:12.960
"Despite being aware of consumers'&nbsp;
problems with the ETF, the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:16.400
continues its practice of steering&nbsp;
consumers to the annual paid monthly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:21.840
plan while obscuring the ETF."
Adobe, naturally, disagreed…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:26.400
amongst other comments.
But that didn't matter.&nbsp;

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:33.680
After multiple years of fighting, Adobe gave in.
They settled for $150 million. $75 million in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.240
penalties. $75 million in free services for&nbsp;
the customers they wronged. And they agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:42.720
to disclose subscription terms upfront —&nbsp;
the thing they'd been hiding all along.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:46.240
Of course, they didn't admit to&nbsp;
any wrongdoing, as expected in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:48.480
a settlement case.
After the verdict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:52.880
Shantanu Narayen, the CEO, resigned.&nbsp;
He'll step down when a successor is found.&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:58.000
The one who pushed for the subscription&nbsp;
model, after 18 years, was leaving.&nbsp;

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:02.800
So — is Adobe's reign finally over?
Not exactly.&nbsp;

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:08.720
Adobe would still likely continue with these&nbsp;
fees, but make them clearer and less hidden.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:13.360
Adobe is making serious money.
Stupid money.&nbsp;

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:20.880
$6.40 billion in revenue, Q1 FY2026. Up 12% from&nbsp;
the same quarter the year before. Operating income&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:29.520
was $2.42 billion. Net income: $1.89 billion.
Adobe's revenue has nearly quintupled since 2015.&nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:34.400
But take a look at this.
Adobe keeps making more money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:40.960
And its stock keeps falling. Right now,&nbsp;
it's the lowest it's been in eight years.&nbsp;

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:45.360
They're no longer a $100 billion company.
As of me writing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:51.040
they have a P/E ratio of about 14.
High margin high-growth software companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:56.480
are usually multiple times higher than that.
And Adobe's has been falling for a long time.&nbsp;

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:00.960
In fact, every time I check it&nbsp;
while writing, it keeps going down.&nbsp;

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:04.640
Revenue up. Earnings up, but, the&nbsp;
amount investors are willing to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:08.640
for those earnings, is falling.
What the heck is going on?

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:14.160
Is it the lawsuit? Losing Figma?
Partially, but there's something else.&nbsp;

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:27.840
Ironically, one of Adobe's biggest bets&nbsp;
is exactly what's got investors worried.&nbsp;

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:32.560
Justice has finally come for Adobe,&nbsp;
and if you like stories like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:39.280
please subscribe! It helps a ton, and we don't&nbsp;
have hidden subscription fees like Adobe does!

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:42.800
Speaking of which, Adobe has&nbsp;
two money engines right now.&nbsp;

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:48.640
The first: Digital Media. Basically Creative&nbsp;
Cloud apps. Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:53.040
but also Document Cloud, which is&nbsp;
basically Acrobat and PDF workflows.&nbsp;

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:57.760
PDFs are everywhere. That part of&nbsp;
the business isn't going anywhere.&nbsp;

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:01.840
In Q1 FY2026, Digital Media&nbsp;
brought in $4.84 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:05.360
Up from $4.39 billion the same quarter last year.&nbsp;

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:10.960
Document Cloud alone hit $843&nbsp;
million — up 17% year over year.&nbsp;

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:17.520
That's huge, but it's not even the whole picture.
The second is Digital Experience — Adobe's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:24.160
marketing and enterprise arm. Analytics,&nbsp;
commerce, customer data tools. In Q1 FY2026,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:29.520
that segment did $1.41 billion. Up&nbsp;
from $1.33 billion the year before.

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:34.640
So Adobe is making more money than&nbsp;
ever. From more places than ever.&nbsp;

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:36.320
Why is the stock still falling?

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:41.520
Losing Figma hurt Adobe in ways the&nbsp;
$1 billion fee doesn't fully capture.&nbsp;

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:46.800
That deal was supposed to be Adobe's move into&nbsp;
collaborative design and product workflows — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:53.120
space it didn't dominate yet. Walking away didn't&nbsp;
just cost a billion, it funded a huge threat.&nbsp;

00:13:53.120 --> 00:14:00.720
But this is much bigger than just Figma.
Both Adobe and Figma are utilizing the same thing.&nbsp;

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:03.520
AI.
In March 2023, Adobe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:10.000
launched Adobe Firefly — its family of generative&nbsp;
AI models, built directly into Creative Cloud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:15.840
Type a prompt, generate an image. Text to image,&nbsp;
text to video, generative fill in Photoshop.&nbsp;

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:20.800
Within a year of its beta launch, users&nbsp;
had generated over 13 billion images with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:24.800
Firefly. Thirteen billion.
Adobe even built a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:28.640
monetisation layer around it.
In other words: Adobe had moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:35.760
from selling software… to selling AI outputs.
This might sound good but this was a huge problem.&nbsp;

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:39.840
Not only were there huge controversies,&nbsp;
like Adobe forcing users to allow their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:44.000
work to be used as training data,
But also requiring many users to grant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:49.680
them full access to users' local content,&nbsp;
and most bafflingly of all, they included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:56.560
that they had a "non-exclusive, worldwide,&nbsp;
royalty-free sublicensable, license, to use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.560 --> 00:15:22.960
reproduce, publicly display", and much more.
This was especially bad for users and companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:27.920
who use NDAs to protect their work, like&nbsp;
those working in the film or commercial space.&nbsp;

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:32.240
The backlash was immense, and&nbsp;
Adobe quickly backpedalled on it.&nbsp;

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:35.600
But, Adobe had created a&nbsp;
bigger problem for themselves.&nbsp;

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:39.920
Investors are increasingly worried that&nbsp;
generative AI won't strengthen Adobe's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:46.080
competitive advantage but do the opposite.
Analysts are concerned AI tools are directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:50.400
challenging Adobe's traditional software&nbsp;
model, and that rivals like Canva and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:56.000
Figma are moving fast — building AI&nbsp;
features directly into their products.&nbsp;

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:02.160
"The market already viewed Adobe as on the wrong&nbsp;
side of the early AI winners and losers, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:08.240
CEO stepping down without a clear succession&nbsp;
plan has simply deepened that skepticism."&nbsp;

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:13.520
Adobe's moat was their massive portfolio,&nbsp;
deep, integrated workflows, those insanely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:17.200
packed tools on top of tools.
Switching from Creative Cloud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:23.200
especially in a business, was quite hard.
But now, with such a big push into AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:29.040
and design getting faster and cheaper because of&nbsp;
AI… Adobe's competitive advantage is shrinking.&nbsp;

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:32.240
Competitors now have an easy&nbsp;
comparison point to them.&nbsp;

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:36.320
No matter what Adobe does, it&nbsp;
can't seem to escape the pressure.&nbsp;

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:41.520
AI, Figma, subscription fees.&nbsp;
Granted, it's all their fault.&nbsp;

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:46.000
Though to be fair — Figma is&nbsp;
feeling it too after their IPO.&nbsp;

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:49.680
But it feels like Adobe's&nbsp;
tight grip might be loosening.&nbsp;

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:52.560
Justice has come at last for Adobe.

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:55.840
They're not the only ones&nbsp;
experiencing AI woes either.&nbsp;

00:16:55.840 --> 00:16:59.920
Microsoft is, weirdly enough,&nbsp;
going through something much worse.&nbsp;

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:03.920
Microsoft's market cap dropped&nbsp;
$500 billion in a single week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:14.016
after they announced more AI spend.
